# Daimler TE30, 1913.
#
# Daimler's 4,963 cc sleeve-valve six, 90 x 130 mm, the 30 hp model of the
# reduced five-model 1913 range. Chassis GBP 650; seven standard bodies GBP 786 to
# GBP 910; the Cranmore landaulette catalogued at GBP 910, GBP 1,100 for the
# surviving car. Three TE30s survive. Daimler built 1,000 cars in 1913 with 5,000
# workers.
# https://www.jaguarheritage.com/car/1913_daimler_te30_cranmore_landaulette/
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Daimler_cars
#
# Colours: Black, Dark Green, Maroon and Grey, as specified for this model.
#
# Weight: 10 in total (black 3, green 3, maroon 2, grey 2). The TE30 is a
# single-year model with three survivors, so the total sits at the bottom of the
# 10-15 band rather than in the middle of it.
#
# Speed 80 km/h: no TE30 maximum speed has been found. 80 km/h (50 mph) is
# derived from the 51.5 mph at 1,800 rpm published for the 1919 30 hp Daimler
# Light Thirty (Motor Sport, May 1961), reduced for the heavier landaulette
# body, and is flagged for review. Comfort 65/428 (line). Classes 2,3,4.
# Dates: 1913 only, with a road tail to about 1938.
#
# Dimensions have not been found for the landaulette body. The model was built
# to these ESTIMATES: about 4.700 m long, 1.750 m wide, 2.100 m high as a
# landaulette, 3.429 m wheelbase. Correct them if a works figure is found.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/daimler-te30.blend. References:
# citycars/references/daimler-te30/ (gitignored). Images via Wikimedia Commons.
#
# Photographs: all four are the Jaguar Daimler Heritage Trust's Cranmore
# landaulette, registration DL 843.
# daimlerte30-a.jpg side view, CC BY 2.0, Marian Byrne,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1913_Daimler_TE30_Cranmore_Landaulette_8996822941.jpg
# daimlerte30-b.jpg front, CC BY-SA 4.0, Calreyn88,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1913_Daimler_TE30_Cranmore_Landaulette_2.jpg
# daimlerte30-c.jpg rear, CC BY 2.0, Marian Byrne,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1913_Daimler_TE30_Cranmore_Landaulette_8998005454.jpg
# daimlerte30-d.jpg rear three-quarter, CC BY 2.0, andreboeni,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daimler_TE30_Cranmore_Landaulette_(1913).jpg

